            <ArticleTitle>Relationship between BCG scar size and asthma in children?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>916-21</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This case control study was conducted to evaluate any association between the BCG scar size and occurrence of asthma among children between 6-14 years of age.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cases consisted of 90 asthmatic children. Control group included 90 non-asthmatic children from the emergency room service of the same hospital. The BCG scar was measured as the average of the transverse and longitudinal diameters.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The results showed that asthmatic subjects have a 3.2 times greater risk exhibiting a scar diameter of less than 5mm than non- asthmatic subjects (CI 95 percent = 1.40 - 7.63 P &lt; 0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It was concluded that asthmatic children and adolescents exhibited a greater frequency of an BCG scar diameter of less than 5mm than non-asthmatics. Clinical significance of this observation is uncertain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
